WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A spent Falcon 9 stage is heading for a lunar impact this week that orbiters will track in real time. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 150. Updated impact coordinates place the roughly 3 tons stage at 93 degrees west, 20 degrees north. NASA and South Korean orbiters will record the flash, crater, and ejecta in real time. Pre-impact imaging allows immediate comparison of regolith properties. The stage will

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:59.900
strike at several kilometers per second. Scientists will study how much ejecta reaches escape velocity during the controlled experiment. Now, shifting focus to deeper cosmic questions. The james web space telescope continues to deliver data that challenges prior models of how galaxies formed in the first few hundred million years after the Big Bang. Observations reveal unexpectedly bright and massive systems at high red shift, forcing revisions to time

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:19.540
lines for star formation and black hole growth. These findings come from deep field exposures that capture light emitted when the universe was less than 500 million years old. Astronomers are now cross checking the results against simulations to understand whether current theories of hierarchical merging need adjustment.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:36.480
Follow-up spectroscopy campaigns are planned to confirm distances and compositions. While one spent rocket stage offers a quick lunar science opportunity, another NASA observatory is forcing slower, deeper revisions to how we think galaxies formed.

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:45.960
High contrast coronographic images from JWST reveal a clear warp in the disk surrounding the young star, TW Hydrae.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:02:07.480
The distortion appears in the inner regions where planets are thought to form. Published results show the warps geometry and suggest possible causes such as an unseen companion or recent dynamical interaction. The nearby system allows detailed study of disk evolution at scales comparable to our own solar systems early stages.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:20.960
A protoplanetary disk around a young star like TW Hydrae can stretch more than 100 times the Earth's sun distance yet still show a distinct bend within the inner few dozen astronomical units.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:31.920
Picture a vast spinning plate of gas and dust the width of a major city's metropolitan area. Now tilt just the central dinner plate sized region by several degrees.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:41.100
That tilt can arise when an unseen planet or stellar companion gravitationally tugs one section of the disk out of alignment with the rest.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:53.580
JWST's recent coronographic images captured exactly this geometry around TW Hydrae, revealing the warp through scattered starlight at near infrared wavelengths.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:03:02.760
The structure matters because it can funnel material inward or outward, changing where planets ultimately form and how much gas they accrete.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:13.000
The open question is whether such warps are common signposts of forming planets or whether they trace rarer dynamical events that only appear briefly.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:21.320
From disks where planets are still forming we turn to one world that has already finished forming and is now being directly imaged.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:34.220
The James Webb Space Telescope obtained direct images of a low mass exoplanet previously difficult to resolve. The observations help characterize the planet's temperature and atmospheric properties.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.700
This target ranks among the lowest mass worlds imaged outside our solar system.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:50.460
Coronographic techniques suppress the host star's light to reveal the companion. Additional epochs are planned to track orbital motion and refine mass estimates.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:58.820
While JWST appears at distant worlds closer to home NASA is preparing the hardware that will return humans to the moon.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:07.660
NASA continues stacking and testing elements at Kennedy Space Center for the Artemis III crude lunar landing targeted for next year.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:15.560
Engineers are verifying connections between the Orion spacecraft, the space launch system rocket, and supporting ground systems.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:21.820
Recent milestones include completion of electrical interface checks and thermal protection inspections.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:29.460
The campaign builds directly on recent Artemis progress by moving from crude training to flight hardware verification.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:37.580
Launch window planning remains active as final integration proceeds. Artemis aims to land on the moon deliberately.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:45.040
From proven endurance on another world we close with two new ground facilities built to test the vehicles that will follow.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:52.760
NASA's ingenuity rotor craft was built to operate for 30 days on Mars yet completed nearly three years of flights.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:59.960
The vehicle performed dozens of sorties that tested rotor dynamics and navigation in the thin Martian atmosphere.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:10.920
Data gathered during those flights informed designs for future aerial scouts. Mission controllers retired the helicopter after rotor damage ended flight capability.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:17.740
The extended performance record now serves as a benchmark for lightweight aerial systems on other worlds.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:23.640
NASA has commissioned a new flight dynamics research facility at Langley Research Center.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:31.600
The laboratory will simulate spacecraft trajectories and attitude control scenarios in greater detail than previous setups.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:40.660
Researchers plan to run tests supporting both crude and robotic missions. Integration with existing simulation tools is underway.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:47.160
The addition strengthens Langley's role in trajectory design for lunar and deep space operations.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:55.940
The new facility at NASA's Langley Research Center provides expanded testing capacity for high-speed vehicles and reentry systems.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:07.520
Construction marks the first edition of this scale since the 1980s. Engineers will use the tunnel to validate aerodynamic models for future spacecraft and hypersonic concepts.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:17.480
Calibration and initial test articles are already scheduled. The capability directly supports ongoing Artemis and commercial crew vehicle development.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:25.200
The Varacy Rubin Observatory produced an exceptionally deep exposure of the cosmos field and surrounding sky.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:30.920
The image reveals thousands of distant galaxies and faint structures across a wide area.

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:36.920
Data from this field will feed into studies of galaxy evolution and dark matter distribution.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:46.540
Early processing shows the telescope's ability to reach faint magnitudes quickly. Further releases from the survey are expected in coming months.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:53.800
Observations of a super-earth exoplanet indicate the presence of an atmosphere through transmission spectroscopy.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:59.720
The data suggests the world retains a gaseous envelope despite its proximity to its star.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:10.340
This finding adds to the small sample of rocky planets with detectable atmospheres. Further transit measurements are needed to identify specific molecular species.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:17.840
The result supports continued efforts to characterize atmospheres on worlds larger than Earth but smaller than Neptune.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:25.920
StratoLaunch's Talon A autonomous vehicle has now flown more than 10 times after its latest air launch test.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:34.980
The craft reaches speeds near Mach 5 during each mission. Data collected during the flights refine guidance and thermal protection systems.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.380
The program demonstrates repeated operation of a reusable hypersonic test bed.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:44.300
Additional flights are scheduled to expand the performance envelope.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:50.520
An image posted for August 3rd shows a meteor streaking through the field of the Laserta Nebula.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:56.460
The brief trail was captured during a long exposure photograph of the faint emission nebula.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.040
Such events illustrate the constant rain of small debris entering Earth's atmosphere.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:07.520
The photo also highlights the nebula's delicate structure against the background stars.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:12.680
Observers can watch for similar alignments during upcoming meteor showers.

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:20.700
Show Gang Park now hosts the Beijing Space Exploration Center featuring exhibits on rocket development and lunar missions.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.220
The facility includes a simulated launch experience and displays of hardware models.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.660
City officials expect the center to support public education and industry outreach.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.460
Construction wrapped ahead of the public opening this month.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:40.540
The venue joins other regional efforts to highlight China's growing space program.

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:50.080
Before we go, keep an eye on the next round of JWST spectroscopy results expected to confirm those early galaxy distances.

